<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Shōtoku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/574">574</a>–<a href="/years/622">622</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Shōtoku Taishi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Shōtoku (574–622) was a regent and statesman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato court</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under Empress Suiko. Tradition credits him with the Seventeen-Article Constitution of 604, the adoption of Chinese-style court ranks, embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the founding of temples such as Hōryū-ji. A great patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he became a revered figure in Japanese religion, though historians debate how much of his record is legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku.jpg" alt="Prince Shōtoku" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sh%C5%8Dtoku_Taishi_Sh%C5%8Dmanky%C5%8D_K%C5%8Dsan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goguryeo monks and artists also travelled to Japan; the monk Hyeja taught <b>Prince Shōtoku</b>, and the Nihon shoki credits the monk Damjing with introducing the making of paper and ink in 610.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prince Shotoku</b> issued a seventeen-article code in 604, and the Taika reforms of 645 began a centralised land and tax system on Chinese lines.</blockquote><a href="/state/yamato">Yamato</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tachibana-dera Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Ikaruga no miya. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.47,135.82&amp;year=605" aria-label="Tachibana-dera Temple on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8002 915 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Prince Shōtoku"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8452 1185h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8452 1185h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.47,135.82&amp;year=605">What was here in 605 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Harmony is to be valued, and an avoidance of wanton opposition to be honoured.&#8221;</p><footer>Seventeen-Article Constitution, 604, article 1 (Nihongi, book 22) &#183; tr. W. G. Aston, 1896 &#183; the constitution the Nihongi attributes to him &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Nihongi/Book_XXII" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Their right is our wrong, and our right is their wrong. We are not unquestionably sages, nor are they unquestionably fools. Both of us are simply ordinary men.&#8221;</p><footer>Seventeen-Article Constitution, 604, article 10 (Nihongi, book 22) &#183; tr. W. G. Aston, 1896 &#183; the constitution the Nihongi attributes to him &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Nihongi/Book_XXII" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Decisions on important matters should not be made by one person alone. They should be discussed with many.&#8221;</p><footer>Seventeen-Article Constitution, 604, article 17 (Nihongi, book 22) &#183; tr. W. G. Aston, 1896 &#183; the constitution the Nihongi attributes to him &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Nihongi/Book_XXII" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…then will the suits of the rich man be like a stone flung into water, while the plaints of the poor will resemble water cast upon a stone.&#8221;</p><footer>Seventeen-Article Constitution, 604, article 5 (Nihongi, book 22) &#183; tr. W. G. Aston, 1896 &#183; the constitution the Nihongi attributes to him &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Nihongi/Book_XXII" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Be not envious. For if we envy others, they in turn will envy us. The evils of envy know no limit.&#8221;</p><footer>Seventeen-Article Constitution, 604, article 14 (Nihongi, book 22) &#183; tr. W. G. Aston, 1896 &#183; the constitution the Nihongi attributes to him &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Nihongi/Book_XXII" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Good faith is the foundation of right. In everything let there be good faith, for in it there surely consists the good and the bad, success and failure.&#8221;</p><footer>Seventeen-Article Constitution, 604, article 9 (Nihongi, book 22) &#183; tr. W. G. Aston, 1896 &#183; the constitution the Nihongi attributes to him &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Nihongi/Book_XXII" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
